# Claim overlap planning seam

Citable spec for `ARCHIMP-S20`. Workers implement this document, not discarded
`ARCHIMP-S15` branches or out-of-repo handoffs.

## Problem

`arm claim` decides overlapping-scope Claims in `cmd/armature/claim.go`: block
a foreign-Worker overlap, dismiss a same-Worker overlap, or force-override.
`internal/claim` only supplies predicates (`ScopesOverlapEx`,
`HasOverlapDismissalNote`). The policy has no module, so tests must cross
Cobra, git, and worktree setup.

The command loop also:

- ranges a Go map, so diagnostic and Op order depend on insertion luck
- returns on the first foreign overlap instead of collecting every block
- can append a same-Worker dismissal note and then fail on a later foreign
  overlap
- ignores `appendOp` errors for overlap notes, so missing evidence does not
  stop the Claim

## Seam

One pure in-process planning module in `internal/claim`. `cmd/armature` adapts
Materialized State into Claim facts and performs stderr writes and Op appends.

Keep `internal/claim` independent of `internal/materialize`. Depguard already
forbids that import. Add no child policy package, writer port, rule interface,
or hypothetical adapter. Existing predicates (`ScopesOverlapEx`,
`IsClaimStale`, `ResolveClaim`, `IsWithinScope`) stay; overlap *policy* moves
behind the new interface.

Names:

```go
type IssueFacts struct {
    Type      string
    Status    string
    ClaimedBy string
    Title     string
    Scope     []string
}

type PlanInput struct {
    TargetID    string
    TargetScope []string
    WorkerID    string
    Force       bool
    Issues      map[string]IssueFacts
    Graph       HierarchyGraph
    PriorOps    []ops.Op
}

type NoteIntent struct {
    IssueID string
    Message string
}

type ClaimPlan struct {
    BlockReasons []string
    Warnings     []string
    Notes        []NoteIntent
}

func PlanClaim(PlanInput) (ClaimPlan, error)
```

`HierarchyGraph` is the existing `Descendants` interface in this package.
Inputs are borrowed and read-only.

## Glossary term

Add to `CONTEXT.md` in the planning task:

**Claim Plan**: The overlap decision for a proposed Claim, produced before any
Op is appended. It names block reasons, warnings, and note intents. Distinct
from the Claim Op itself.
_Avoid_: Resolver, overlap check, claim result

## Behavior

`PlanClaim` owns all of the following.

1. Empty `TargetID`, empty `WorkerID`, or a nil `Graph` is an input error.
   Never panic. A nil `Issues` map or nil `PriorOps` is a valid empty
   collection and yields an empty plan when the rest of the input is valid.
2. Sort candidate Issue IDs before evaluation. Map insertion order must not
   affect diagnostics or note order.
3. Ignore the target itself.
4. Only another Task in `claimed` or `in-progress` competes. Stories, epics,
   features, bugs, and non-active Tasks do not.
5. Overlap uses `ScopesOverlapEx` so ancestor and descendant pairs remain
   excluded in both directions.
6. Diagnostics name the conflicting Issue ID, title, type, status, and Claimed
   By. Empty Claimed By renders as `unknown`.
7. Exact same-Worker overlap is a dismissal, including when `Force` is set.
8. Deduplicate dismissal evidence by target Issue, conflicting Issue, and
   current Worker identity. A prior `OpNote` counts when it targets the
   claiming Issue, carries the canonical dismissal message for that pair, and
   was written by this Worker.
9. Without `Force`, evaluate every candidate and return every sorted
   foreign-Worker block reason. A blocked plan is atomic: `Warnings` and
   `Notes` are empty, including no same-Worker dismissal notes.
10. With `Force`, return a warning and two reciprocal note intents for every
    foreign-Worker overlap, in candidate-ID order. Same-Worker overlaps remain
    dismissals.
11. Canonical messages, preserved:
    - dismissal note: `Serial claim: scope overlap with {id} (same worker, dismissed)`
    - force notes: `Scope overlap with {id} detected at claim time` on each
      of the two Issues
    - reason text: `scope overlap with {id} ({title}), a {type} {status} held by {holder}`

Force notes are not deduplicated. Dismissal notes are.

## Command adapter

`cmd/armature` maps Materialized State into `PlanInput` using `Claimed By`
(not Assignment). It passes the existing hierarchy graph and the raw Op log
already loaded for this command.

- If `PlanClaim` returns an error: print it, write nothing, do not Claim.
- If `BlockReasons` is non-empty: print each as `Error:` on stderr, return
  `cannot claim {id}: {reason} — use --force to override` for each reason
  (existing phrasing), write nothing, do not create a worktree, do not append
  a Claim Op.
- If `Warnings` is non-empty: print each as `Warning:` on stderr.
- Persist `Notes` with `appendLowStakesOps` so the group is
  Introduction-checked together and append errors are returned. If that batch
  fails, the Claim does not proceed: no Claim Op, no worktree.
- Only then run the existing exclude / Claim-token / `appendHighStakesOp` /
  worktree path.

Do not ignore overlap-note errors. Do not append overlap notes one-by-one with
`appendOp`. Do not change lock ordering, worktree provisioning, claim-token
rollback, or parent auto-advance.

## Tests

Prefer `package claim_test` against the exported interface:

- invalid input returns an error and never panics
- nil `Issues` and nil `PriorOps` produce an empty plan for otherwise valid input
- target, Story, and non-active Tasks are ignored
- claimed and in-progress Tasks compete
- ancestor and descendant overlaps are excluded in both directions
- same-Worker dismissal and Worker-specific deduplication
- empty Claimed By renders as `unknown`
- every foreign conflict is returned, sorted, without `Force`
- blocked mixed overlaps produce no partial note intents
- `Force` produces a warning plus reciprocal notes for every foreign conflict
- same-Worker overlaps remain dismissals under `Force`
- equivalent maps with different insertion orders produce identical plans
- inputs remain unchanged

Keep thin Cobra tests for Materialized State adaptation, stderr labels, exact
Op targets and order, evidence-write failures, and the invariant that a
blocked Claim creates no worktree and no Claim Op.

New tests that verify this spec use `_REQ_ARCHIMP_S20_T1` or
`_REQ_ARCHIMP_S20_T2` in the function name.

## Split

Two Tasks, in order:

1. Pure module, glossary term, and `claim_test` coverage. Command still uses
   the old loop until Task 2 lands.
2. Command adaptation and Cobra tests. Blocked by Task 1.

## Out of scope

- Recreating `ARCHIMP-S15` / `ARCHIMP-S15-T1` / `ARCHIMP-S15-T2`, or citing
  discarded branches as implementation sources
- Importing `internal/materialize` into `internal/claim`
- Splitting staleness, heartbeat, or race-winner out of `internal/claim`
- Promotion redesign

## How `ARCHIMP` closes

`ARCHIMP-S15` stays `cancelled`. This replacement is a new child of `ARCHIMP`.
Materialization rollup promotes a parent only when every child is `merged`.
Cancelled children count as unmerged, so a merged replacement will not
auto-promote `ARCHIMP` while `ARCHIMP-S15` remains cancelled.

That is expected. `ARCHIMP` is an open epic that still collects architecture
work. Closing it is a later, explicit human transition — not a side effect of
this story, and not a force of epic status after a cancelled child.
